    P("connorsrsi-pullback", "ConnorsRSI Pullback", "ConnorsRSI (3, 2, 100) pullback strategy", "reversion", ["larry-connors", "cesar-alvarez", "matt-radtke"], "D", FM.connorsrsi_pullback,
      "Buy a stock in a strong trend after a sharp one-day sell-off pushes ConnorsRSI below 10, and sell when it recovers above 70.",
      ["ConnorsRSI averages three 0 to 100 readings: a 3-day RSI of the close, a 2-day RSI of the current up or down streak, and where today's 1-day return ranks among the previous 100.",
       "In at the close when the 10-day ADX (a gauge of trend strength) is above 30, the day's low is at least 4% below the prior close, the close is in the bottom 25% of the day's range, and ConnorsRSI is below 10.",
       "Out at the close when ConnorsRSI is above 70."],
      ["The guidebook buys the next day with a limit order 2% to 10% below the signal close. Be The Puck buys at the signal close, a worse price than the book's.",
       "The guidebook tests a range of settings. Its worked example uses a 4% drop, the bottom 25% of the range, ConnorsRSI below 10 and an exit above 70, and those are used here.",
       "The $5 minimum price and 250,000-share average volume filters are dropped. As in the guidebook, there is no 200-day average filter.",
       "Days where the high equals the low give no entry."],
      "An Introduction to ConnorsRSI, Connors Research guidebook (2012)",
      sources=[("An Introduction to ConnorsRSI (Connors, Alvarez & Radtke, 2012)", "https://c.mql5.com/forextsd/forum/119/connorsrsi-pullbacks-guidebook.pdf"),
               ("StockCharts ChartSchool: ConnorsRSI", "https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/connorsrsi")]),
    P("laguerre-rsi", "Laguerre RSI", "Ehlers Laguerre RSI 0.2/0.8", "reversion", ["john-ehlers"], "D", FM.laguerre_rsi,
      "Ehlers' fast RSI, built from four stages of a Laguerre filter instead of a fixed look-back, so it reacts with little lag. Buy when it climbs back above 0.2, sell when it drops back below 0.8.",
      ["The Laguerre RSI runs from 0 to 1, with a damping factor (gamma) of 0.5.",
       "In when it crosses up through 0.2.", "Out when it crosses down through 0.8."],
      ["Gamma 0.5 is the value in Ehlers' published code. He says traders can adjust it.",
       "The filter starts from the first close.",
       "There is no stop: the rule can hold through a long decline."],
      "'Time Warp - Without Space Travel' (MESA Software); Cybernetic Analysis for Stocks and Futures (2004)",
      sources=[("Ehlers: Time Warp - Without Space Travel", "https://mesasoftware.com/papers/TimeWarp.pdf"),
               ("Wiley: Cybernetic Analysis for Stocks and Futures, contents", "https://gov.wiley.com/WileyCDA/WileyTitle/productCd-0471463078,descCd-tableOfContents.html")]),
    P("stochrsi-20-80", "StochRSI 20/80", "Chande & Kroll StochRSI 0.2/0.8", "reversion", ["tushar-chande", "stanley-kroll"], "D", FM.stochrsi,
      "The stochastic formula applied to the 14-day RSI: where today's RSI sits in its own 14-day range. It reaches overbought and oversold far more often than RSI does. Buy out of oversold, sell out of overbought.",
      ["StochRSI = (RSI − lowest RSI of the last 14 days) ÷ (highest − lowest), from 0 to 1.",
       "In when StochRSI crosses up through 0.2.", "Out when it crosses down through 0.8."],
      ["Chande and Kroll defined 0.8 and 0.2 as overbought and oversold levels, not trading rules. Buying the cross back up through 0.2 and selling the cross down through 0.8 mirrors Be The Puck's RSI 30/70 play.",
       "Raw StochRSI is used. Many platforms smooth it with a 3-day average.",
       "There is no stop: the rule can hold through a long decline."],
      "The New Technical Trader (1994)",
      sources=[("StockCharts ChartSchool: StochRSI", "https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/stochrsi")]),
    P("ibs-reversion", "IBS Reversion", "Internal bar strength below 0.2", "reversion", ["alexander-pagonidis"], "D", FM.ibs,
      "Internal bar strength is where the close lands in the day's range. A close near the low has tended to bounce the next day, so buy those closes and hold for one day.",
      ["IBS = (close − low) ÷ (high − low). 0 means it closed at the low, 1 at the high.",
       "In at the close when IBS is below 0.2.", "Out at the next close unless IBS is below 0.2 again."],
      ["The documented effect is about the next day's return, so the hold is one day. A common variant holds until IBS closes above 0.8.",
       "Pagonidis studied equity ETFs. Single stocks are an extension.",
       "Days where the high equals the low give no signal."],
      "'The IBS Effect: Mean Reversion in Equity ETFs', NAAIM Founders Award entry (2014)",
      sources=[("arXiv 2306.12434: Internal Bar Strength for trading country ETFs", "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.12434"),
               ("NAAIM: Founders Award paper list 2009-2018", "https://www.naaim.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/ALL-founders-award-paper-list_2009-2018_web.pdf")]),
    P("connors-percent-b", "Connors %b", "Connors %b three-day oversold", "reversion", ["larry-connors", "cesar-alvarez"], "D", FM.connors_pctb,
      "In a long-term uptrend, buy after the close has sat near or below the bottom of its Bollinger Bands for three days running, and sell once it gets near the top.",
      ["%b shows where the close sits in its Bollinger Bands: 0 at the lower band, 1 at the upper.",
       "In at the close when the stock is above its 200-day average and %b has closed below 0.2 on each of the last 3 days.",
       "Out at the close when %b is above 0.8."],
      ["None of the sources seen give the band settings. Bollinger's standard 20-day bands at 2 standard deviations are assumed.",
       "Connors' aggressive version adds a second unit on a lower close. Be The Puck holds one unit.",
       "Tested by the authors on ETFs. Single stocks are an extension."],
      "High Probability ETF Trading (2009)", flag="Band settings unconfirmed",
      sources=[("Quantified Strategies: Larry Connors %b strategy", "https://quantifiedstrategies.substack.com/p/larry-connors-b-strategy-bollinger-ab6"),
               ("TradingView: Larry Connors %b Strategy (EdgeTools)", "https://jp.tradingview.com/script/yXkWPSlU"),
               ("StockCharts ChartSchool: %B Indicator", "https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/b-indicator")]),
    P("fisher-transform", "Fisher Transform", "Ehlers Fisher transform trigger cross", "reversion", ["john-ehlers"], "D", FM.fisher,
      "Ehlers stretches price's place in its 10-day range so that turning points stand out sharply. Hold while the Fisher line is above its own value a day earlier.",
      ["Take the midpoint of each day's high and low, place it in its 10-day range on a −1 to +1 scale, smooth it, and apply the Fisher transform, which stretches values near the edges.",
       "In when the Fisher line crosses above its trigger, which is yesterday's Fisher value.",
       "Out when it crosses below."],
      ["Ehlers plots the line and its one-day-old copy as a crossover system for turning points but gives no formal entry or exit. Buying the up-cross and selling the down-cross is Be The Puck's reading.",
       "As in his code, the scaled value is capped at ±0.999 and the smoothing starts from zero."],
      "'Using the Fisher Transform', Stocks & Commodities (November 2002)",
      sources=[("Ehlers: Using The Fisher Transform, Stocks & Commodities V.20:11", "https://c.mql5.com/3/133/Using_The_Fisher_Transform.pdf"),
               ("Wiley: Cybernetic Analysis for Stocks and Futures, contents", "https://gov.wiley.com/WileyCDA/WileyTitle/productCd-0471463078,descCd-tableOfContents.html")]),
    P("short-term-reversal", "1-Month Reversal", "Short-term reversal (last month's laggard)", "reversion", ["jegadeesh-lehmann"], "M", FM.st_reversal,
      "Last month's laggards tend to bounce the next month. Hold the stock for the month after one in which it trailed SPY.",
      ["In at month-end when the stock's return for the month just ended is below SPY's.",
       "Out at month-end when it matched or beat SPY."],
      ["Jegadeesh ranked all stocks by last month's return. Trailing SPY stands in for being one of the losers.",
       "On SPY itself, a down month stands in for trailing the market.",
       "Lehmann's version uses weekly returns. Only the monthly one is tested here.",
       "Returns include dividends, as everywhere on Be The Puck. A price-only index, such as the S&P 500 index itself, trails SPY in most months for that reason alone.",
       "Signals are acted on the first trading day of the next month."],
      "Jegadeesh, Journal of Finance (1990); Lehmann, Quarterly Journal of Economics (1990)", relative=True,
      sources=[("Alpha Architect: Short-term return reversal", "https://alphaarchitect.com/quantitative-momentum-research-short-term-return-reversal/"),
               ("RePEc/IDEAS: Journal of Finance 45(3) 881-98 (1990)", "https://ideas.repec.org/r/bla/jfinan/v45y1990i3p881-98.html"),
               ("NBER Working Paper 2533 (Lehmann)", "https://www.nber.org/papers/w2533.pdf")]),

