    P("on-balance-volume", "On-Balance Volume", "Granville's on-balance volume trend", "volume", ["joseph-granville"], "D", F.obv,
      "A running total of volume: add it on up days, subtract it on down days. Rising OBV says buyers are accumulating.",
      ["In when OBV is above its 20-day average.", "Out when it is below."],
      ["The OBV is Granville's; the 20-day signal line is Be The Puck's. He read OBV's trend and divergences by eye."],
      "Granville's New Key to Stock Market Profits (1963)"),
    P("chaikin-money-flow", "Chaikin Money Flow", "Chaikin money flow", "volume", ["marc-chaikin"], "D", F.cmf,
      "Weighs each day's volume by where the close lands in the day's range. Positive means buying pressure.",
      ["In when the 21-day CMF rises above +0.05.", "Out when it falls below −0.05. In between, the rule keeps its last call."],
      ["The ±0.05 buffer is StockCharts' suggested filter against whipsaws."], "Marc Chaikin (date unverified)"),
    P("money-flow-index", "Money Flow Index", "Money Flow Index 20/80", "volume", ["quong-soudack"], "D", F.mfi,
      "RSI with volume added. Buy when it climbs out of oversold (20), sell when it drops out of overbought (80).",
      ["In when the 14-day MFI crosses up through 20.", "Out when it crosses down through 80."],
      ["The creators also stress divergences, which this rule ignores."], "Technical Analysis of Stocks & Commodities, March 1989"),

    P("negative-volume-index", "Negative Volume Index", "Fosback's Negative Volume Index above its one-year average", "volume",
      ["norman-fosback", "paul-dysart"], "D", FV.nvi,
      "A price index that moves only on days when volume shrinks, on the theory that quiet days show what informed investors are doing. Hold the stock while that index is above its one-year average.",
      ["The index starts at 1,000. On a day when volume is lower than the day before, it moves by that day's percentage price change. On other days it stays put.",
       "In when the index closes above its 255-day exponential average (Fosback's one-year average).",
       "Out when it closes below that average."],
      ["Fosback studied the index for the whole market. Running it on one stock's own price and volume follows modern charting practice (StockCharts, Fidelity).",
       "The one-year average is a 255-day exponential average, as on StockCharts. The type of average in Fosback's original test was not found.",
       "Daily changes compound, as in Fosback's formula.",
       "The rule needs about a year of history before its first call."],
      "Paul Dysart (1936); Norman Fosback, Stock Market Logic (1976)",
      sources=[("StockCharts ChartSchool: Negative Volume Index (NVI)", "https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/negative-volume-index-nvi"),
               ("Fidelity Learning Center: Negative Volume Index", "https://www.fidelity.com/learning-center/trading-investing/technical-analysis/technical-indicator-guide/nvi"),
               ("Wikipedia: Negative volume index", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negative_volume_index")],
      warm=1500),
    P("force-index", "Force Index", "Elder's 13-day Force Index zero-line switch", "volume", ["alexander-elder"], "D", FV.force_index,
      "Each day's price change times its volume, smoothed over 13 days. Elder takes a value above zero to mean the bulls control the trend.",
      ["The 1-day Force Index is today's close minus yesterday's close, times today's volume.",
       "In when its 13-day exponential average is above zero.",
       "Out when it is below zero."],
      ["Elder uses the 13-day Force Index to read the intermediate trend and a 2-day version to time entries inside his Triple Screen. Using the 13-day zero line on its own is Be The Puck's simplification.",
       "There is no buffer around zero, so the call can flip often when the line hugs zero."],
      "Trading for a Living (1993)",
      sources=[("StockCharts ChartSchool: Force Index", "https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/force-index"),
               ("Incredible Charts: Force Index", "https://incrediblecharts.com/indicators/force_index.php"),
               ("Traders.com (Working Money, 2005): Paolo Pezzutti on the Force Index", "https://technical.traders.com/tradersonline/display.asp?art=2648")]),
    P("buff-averages", "Buff Averages", "Dormeier's 5/20-day volume-weighted moving average crossover", "volume", ["buff-dormeier"], "D", FV.buff,
      "Two moving averages that give heavy-volume days more weight, 5 and 20 days long. Hold while the fast one is above the slow one.",
      ["A volume-weighted average adds up each day's close times its volume and divides by the total volume, so busy days count more.",
       "In when the 5-day volume-weighted average is above the 20-day one.",
       "Out when it is below."],
      ["Dormeier's 2001 test also went short on the down-cross. Be The Puck holds T-bills instead.",
       "Signals are taken on the close of the crossover day."],
      "Technical Analysis of Stocks & Commodities, February 2001 ('Buff Up Your Moving Averages')",
      sources=[("Traders.com: Dormeier, 'Buff Up Your Moving Averages' (S&C, February 2001)", "https://traders.com/Documentation/FEEDbk_docs/2001/02/Abstracts_new/Dormeier/Dormeier.html")]),
    P("chaikin-oscillator", "Chaikin Oscillator", "Chaikin Oscillator zero-line switch (3- and 10-day EMAs of the A/D line)", "volume", ["marc-chaikin"], "D", FV.chaikin_osc,
      "Measures the momentum of Chaikin's Accumulation/Distribution line. Hold while it is above zero, when buying pressure has the upper hand.",
      ["The Accumulation/Distribution line is a running total of volume, weighted by where each close sits in the day's range: fully added at the high, fully subtracted at the low.",
       "The oscillator is the line's 3-day exponential average minus its 10-day one.",
       "In when the oscillator is above zero.",
       "Out when it is below zero."],
      ["StockCharts also reads divergences. This rule uses only the zero line.",
       "A day with no range (high equals low) adds no volume to the line."],
      "Marc Chaikin (date unverified)",
      sources=[("StockCharts ChartSchool: Chaikin Oscillator", "https://chartschool.stockcharts.com/table-of-contents/technical-indicators-and-overlays/technical-indicators/chaikin-oscillator"),
               ("Wikipedia: Accumulation/distribution index (Chaikin oscillator)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accumulation/distribution_index")]),
    P("volume-flow-indicator", "Volume Flow Indicator", "Katsanos' Volume Flow Indicator (VFI) zero-line switch", "volume", ["markos-katsanos"], "D", FV.vfi,
      "A refined on-balance volume that ignores tiny price moves and caps freak volume spikes. Hold while it is above zero.",
      ["Each day's volume counts as buying if the typical price (the average of high, low and close) rose by more than a small cutoff, as selling if it fell by more than the cutoff, and as nothing otherwise. The cutoff is 0.2 times the stock's recent daily volatility.",
       "Volume is capped at 2.5 times its 130-day average. The VFI is the 130-day sum of the signed volume divided by average volume, smoothed over 3 days.",
       "In when the VFI is above zero.",
       "Out when it is below zero."],
      ["Settings are Katsanos' published daily defaults: 130 days, cutoff 0.2, volume cap 2.5, smoothing 3. The 30-day volatility window comes from the standard published code.",
       "Average volume is the 130-day average up to the day before, as in the published code.",
       "Katsanos also stresses divergences, which are not used."],
      "Technical Analysis of Stocks & Commodities, June 2004 ('Using Money Flow To Stay With The Trend')",
      sources=[("Traders.com: Traders' Tips, June 2004 (VFI code and parameters)", "https://traders.com/Documentation/FEEDbk_docs/2004/06/TradersTips/TradersTips.html"),
               ("ProRealCode: Volume Flow Indicator (Katsanos, 2004) code and signal description", "https://prorealcode.com/prorealtime-indicators/volume-flow-indicator"),
               ("TradingView: VFI script (daily vs intraday defaults)", "https://www.tradingview.com/script/kPeyW7i5/")]),
    P("klinger-oscillator", "Klinger Oscillator", "Klinger Volume Oscillator (34/55-day, 13-day trigger)", "volume", ["stephen-klinger"], "D", FV.klinger,
      "Volume signed by the direction of the day's typical price and weighted by how long that direction has lasted, run through 34 and 55-day averages. Hold while it is above its 13-day trigger line.",
      ["Volume force is each day's volume, counted as plus when high + low + close rises and minus when it falls, and weighted more heavily the longer the current run of same-direction days has lasted.",
       "The oscillator is the 34-day exponential average of volume force minus the 55-day one. The trigger is its 13-day exponential average.",
       "In when the oscillator is above the trigger.",
       "Out when it is below."],
      ["Klinger put most weight on extreme readings that reverse and on divergences. The trigger-line cross is the standard published signal (CQG and others).",
       "Some versions drop the absolute value in the volume-force formula, which flips its sign. The absolute-value form is used here.",
       "The range count starts on the first day of history, and days with no range at all get no volume force."],
      "Technical Analysis of Stocks & Commodities, December 1997 ('Identifying Trends With Volume Analysis')",
      sources=[("CQG News: Klinger Volume Oscillator (periods and crossover rule)", "https://news.cqg.com/node/1508"),
               ("One Trade Journal: Klinger oscillator and volume force formula", "https://onetradejournal.com/indicators/klinger-oscillator"),
               ("Traders.com: Klinger article excerpt (December 1997)", "https://traders.com/Documentation/FEEDbk_docs/1997/12/Abstracts_new/Klinger9712/Kinger.html")]),

